Pandas use the notes from https://github.com/rajukan/stats-ml-notes/blob/main/books/Smarter_pandas_workshop_safari.pdf for some best practices
My goal is not to memorize pandas APIs. I want to develop senior-level instincts: knowing which operation to use, understanding what pandas is doing with indexes and data types, writing idiomatic/vectorized code, recognizing inefficient approaches, and being able to explain tradeoffs during an interview.
Organize the learning priorities in this style:
Python foundation for pandas: list/dict aggregation, comprehensions, lambda/functions, iteration, string parsing, data transformation, sorting, filtering, and understanding Python behavior — I need to be strong enough in pure Python to understand what pandas is replacing and when pandas is the better abstraction
Pandas core mental model: Series vs DataFrame, indexes, index alignment, label-based vs positional selection (.loc vs .iloc), boolean masking, slicing/filtering, broadcasting, and understanding the restricted computation domain rather than treating pandas as “Python with tables”
Data loading and construction: read_csv/read_parquet, DataFrame constructors, from_records, handling nested JSON with json_normalize, text vs binary formats, and understanding how input formats affect resulting dtypes
Data cleaning: column-name normalization, mixed types, dtype coercion, nullable dtypes, missing data, duplicates, unnecessary rows/columns, entity normalization, string operations, and constructing clean pipelines with assign/mask rather than unnecessary manual loops
Grouped operations: groupby, agg, transform, filter, and apply — understand the difference between N×G → G×G and N×G → N operations, prefer specific verbs such as agg/transform over apply when possible, avoid one function call per group, and be able to recognize when a groupby solution can be expressed using built-in/vectorized operations
Reshaping: understand wide vs long data and become fluent with melt, pivot, pivot_table, stack, unstack, lreshape, and wide_to_long — focus on recognizing the shape transformation required rather than memorizing isolated syntax
Multiple DataFrames: merge vs join vs concat, including left/right/inner/outer/cross joins, index-based operations, column-based operations, and understanding alignment of indexes and columns
Datetime and time-series operations: to_datetime, datetime accessors, Timedelta, Period, diff, date filtering, resampling, and time-based aggregation
Strings and categoricals: .str operations, string dtypes, category dtype, factorization, categorical codes/categories, and understanding memory/performance implications of strings vs categoricals
Performance instincts: understand why pandas/numpy can outperform Python loops, why repeatedly crossing between Python objects and pandas/numpy is undesirable, memory footprint, homogeneous dtypes, nullable dtypes, categorical storage, vectorized operations, chunksize/chunk processing, and how to reason about performance without blindly optimizing
Senior-level problem solving: for every problem, first identify the data shape, required transformation, grouping/alignment behavior, and expected output shape; then choose the most appropriate pandas operation
Interview pattern: whenever possible, show how the same problem would be solved using pure Python and then pandas. Make me understand what pandas abstraction replaces the Python loop/dictionary/list logic and why the pandas solution is preferable or not preferable
Practice style: give me realistic ML/data-engineering interview problems rather than trivia. Problems should involve messy data, filtering, aggregation, grouping, joining, reshaping, timestamps, missing values, duplicate records, categorical values, and large datasets. Avoid overly artificial exercises.

